> ===
> The Macroeconomic Case for Adaptation Investment
> Grantham Research Institute on Climate Change and the Environment
> London School of Economics
> Materials for Systemiq Synthesis Report
> Macroeconomic Impacts
> For macroeconomic and fiscal impacts, we synthesise the results of several studies, and identify areas of common work on outcomes of interest. Our evidence base includes 15 studies on impacts of GDP, 12 studies on labour productivity, and three studies on total factor productivity.
> Using a subset of the studies concerned with top-down macroeconomic impacts, we look at impacts of climate change on GDP at the national level. These results are based on a meta-analysis of 85 econometric models from 15 macroeconomic studies of the impacts of temperature on GDP growth. All of these studies use panel econometric methods to causally identify the effects of temperature shocks in the historical record. Models are weighted by their ability to project future losses. Estimates also include the effects of tipping points on global temperatures from Dietz et al. (2021), market losses from sea-level rise and storm surge from Depsky et al. (2023), and trade spillovers from Rising et al. (2023). Only historically observed adaptation is included.
> As a meta-analysis of macro-econometric results, several important features are missing, generally due to the limitations of current econometric methods. Importantly, these models do not account for non-market welfare losses, such as impacts on health and the biosphere, which are expected to be large; this is missing because it is outside the intended scope of these analysis. Total economic damages (including market and non-market losses) are expected to be considerably higher than these estimated market losses. In addition, our synthesis of the results misses (1) impacts that are not strongly driven by temperature shocks, such as slow onset effects, water supply and demand impacts, and other extreme events, such as surface and river floods, droughts, and wildfire; (2) regional-scale tipping points such as AMOC and sea-level rise tipping points; and (3) future adaptation and the results of broader changes in the economy. These aspects are each included in some form in some macroeconometric studies, but we do not project these aspects of the models in our reanalysis.
> We project these under the high emissions scenario SSP3-7.0, and the low emissions scenario SSP1-2.6. SSP3-7.0 is considered a “baseline” scenario in the IPCC AR6, along with SSP5-8.5, but SSP3-7.0 was selected because the emissions in SSP5-8.5 are likely to be much higher than what current policies can produce. The analysis is performed from 1940 to 2100, with an emphasis on 2050, to draw out medium-term risks. Results are reported as 20-year averages relative to 2040 – 2059.
> SSP3-7.0 which has global temperatures 2.5 °C [IQR 2.2 – 2.8 °C] above pre-industrial by 2050 and 3.9 °C [3.5 – 4.3 °C] by 2100. This high emissions scenario results in much higher damages than the low emissions SSP1-2.6 scenario, which has 2 °C [1.8 – 2.3 °C] warming by 2050. These warming levels are higher than the results from the IPCC for the same scenarios because of the effect of tipping points. The average person lives in a country that is 2.7 °C warming than a 1940 – 1960 baseline under our high-emissions scenario.
> Under the high emissions scenario, the average person lives in a country that will be 9% poorer, as a best estimate, in 2050 than it would have been without climate change. The greatest losses are across low-income countries and across the tropics. In particular, sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia face the largest losses, with some countries expected to lose 20% of their GDP due to climate change by 2050.
> Percentage losses in GDP in 2050 under High Emissions
>  
> Colours and labels show the loss in GDP due to warming from 1960 to 2050, under an SSP3-7.0 scenario, as a percent of each country’s counterfactual GDP without climate change. Countries for which the interquartile range of uncertainty crosses 0 are shown with a diagonal pattern and faded labels. Not all countries are labelled, to reduce visual clutter.
> 
> There is a strong income gradient in these losses, with climate change reinforcing existing inequalities. Least developed countries are projected to be already 10% [5 – 15%] poorer due to climate change today, 14% in 2050, and 20% by 2075. 
> Percent losses in GDP by Economic Group
>  
> Loss in GDP due to warming since 1960, under an SSP3-7.0 scenario, by economic group and year. Error bars show the interquartile range.
> 
> Percent losses in GDP by UNFCCC Party Group
>  
> Loss in GDP due to warming since 1960, under an SSP3-7.0 scenario, by UNFCCC party group and year. Error bars show the interquartile range.
> 
> As mentioned above, total welfare losses are expected to be much higher than GDP losses. We use a meta-analysis of 38 studies of damage estimates (Howard & Sterner 2025) to evaluate welfare impacts along our standard high-emissions scenario and for the year 2050. These damages are reported as GDP-equivalent losses—that is, the amount of consumption loss that produces the same welfare loss. We use the preferred synthetic damage function from Howard & Sterner and the underlying results on the components damages that make it up.
> According to the Howard & Sterner model, total damages in 2050 reach 14% [IQR 8 – 19%]. Almost 50% of damages are attributable to catastrophic impacts. The definition of catastrophic damages varies across the underlying studies in the meta-analysis, but generally represents tipping points or other uncertain thresholds of economic risk. A third of damages is attributable to the effects of climate change on economic growth rates, rather than just considering the immediate impacts in a given year.
> Welfare losses in GDP-equivalent terms in 2050
>  
> Waterfall plot of the damages from Howard & Sterner (2025). Each column shows a component of the total and its corresponding uncertainty (shown as the interquartle range). 
>  
> We estimate labour productivity loss in the future by synthesising 12 studies that report national, regional, or global labour productivity loss in percent terms, under a current policies scenario. Studies range in their inclusion of adaptation: Some macro-modeling studies (e.g., World Bank 2022) include autonomous adaptation (actions taken by the agents individually) but exclude adaptation policies in the results presented here (adaptation costs and benefits are studied in the adaptation results section), while such others rely on experimental studies that include no adaptation effects. Econometric studies include autonomous adaptation, but in some cases also represent reductions in vulnerability as regions become richer or experience more heat (e.g., Rode et al. 2022).
> Labour productivity is expected to be reduced in much of the world, with parts of Africa and Asia experiencing over 5% reductions by 2050. There is considerable uncertainty, with 1-in-20 losses up to 4 times higher than expected losses.
> Total factor productivity (TFP) growth is expected to be lowered by climate change. This is based on combining the results of three econometric studies. These include autonomous adaptation. The largest expected reductions in growth are in Africa, at 0.05 percentage points lower per year. There is considerable uncertainty around these numbers, however, with a 1-in-4 chance that TFP is 0.3 percentage points lower per year across much of the world, which would result in a 7% loss in TFP by 2050.
> Labour Productivity and Total Factor Productivity Impacts from Climate Change in 2050.
>  
> Expected impact on labour productivity and total factor productivity growth, due to climate change, from a synthesis of studies. Studies used different scenarios, but only scenarios that can be considered middle-of-the-road, current policies, or high-emissions scenarios are included. Interquartile ranges (error bars) cover both within-study and across-study uncertainty.
> 
> Fiscal Impacts
> Physical climate risks may increasingly affect sovereign spreads and credit ratings, especially in vulnerable economies. We analyse results from Klusak et al. (2023) to evaluate these impacts and find that all regions of the world are at risk of downgrades. In contrast to our macroeconomic results, we also find that the greatest impacts of climate change on sovereign credit ratings fall on upper middle income and high-income countries, across all regions. This is likely driven by the greater potential reductions in credit ratings for higher income countries. Some highly vulnerable countries do not have a credit rating. These results assume that countries adapt to warmer temperatures over a 30-year period, and reductions in GDP (the major channel for sovereign credit changes in Klusak et al.’s model) are then driven by deviations between this moving 30-year climate and observed temperatures.
> It should be noted that the benefits of adaptation are inadequately integrated into creditworthiness evaluations and studies of the effects of climate change, and that this omission has consequences for the ability of countries to secure the needed investments.
> 
> Sovereign Credit Downgrading across regions and income groups in 2050
>  
> Impact of climate change on sovereign credit ratings, from Klusak et al. (2023), evaluated under our standard SSP3-7.0 scenario for 2050.
> 
> Adaptation Results
> We collect adaptation costs, benefits, and economic returns from 75 studies, and are able to combine results from 22 studies into three consistent metrics of the benefits of adaptation: Adaptation ratios (e.g., World Bank 2024), Economic benefit-cost ratios (e.g., World Economic Forum 2024), and Economic internal rates of return (e.g., World Resources Institute 2025). 
> Adaptation benefit (AR) is the portion of losses that are reduced due to adaptation. The economic benefit-cost ratio (BCR) is the ratio of the discounted sum of benefits divided by the discounted sum of costs. Economic rate of return (EIRR) is equal to the discount rate at which the net-present value of benefits minus costs is zero. These three measures differ along two key dimensions. (1) Costs are included in BCR and EIRR, but not in AR. (2) While BCR embeds a particular discount rate, EIRR treats the discount rate as the main outcome of interest. However, high AR can contribute to high BCR and EIRR, and actions with high BCR generally have high EIRR and visa versa.
> Our approach focuses on the macroeconomic perspective for adaptation benefits. We synthesise results from multiple studies, and we infer metrics where the component outcomes are reported. Where studies provide project-specific results, we combine these to the sectoral level within a given country. Other studies perform macroeconomic analysis directly, and some of these explore the full range of adaptation needs. These results are shown as histograms over sectors below.
> We find that adaptation actions are effective, and reduce impacts by half on average, with the highest reduction potential for projects that deliver public goods, technological solutions, and monetary transfers. 
> Nearly all of the economic benefit-cost ratios assessed for macroeconomic adaptation actions were found to be above 1, with a median value of 4 times more benefits than costs.
> The median economic internal rate of return across the database is 25%, suggesting very high returns for adaptation, although this is primarily based on ex ante studies.
> It should be noted that many of the underlying results include economic benefits beyond the direct reduction in losses. However, across results where both avoided losses and other aspects of a Triple Dividend from adaptation were reported, we find that the majority of reported adaptation benefits are avoided losses. It is also worth noting that most benefits accrue to individuals, so that fiscal capture of these benefits is challenging.
> Histograms of Economic Returns to Adaptation
>  
> Histograms of the economic returns to adaptation. Each histogram shows individual sector assessments, combined across projects and solution types. Definitions of the benefit measures are given in the text. Values are shown on a log scale.
